      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n**Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail**\n\nProduct roadmaps are meant to align teams, set expectations, and guide execution toward valuable outcomes. Yet most fail—not because planning is inherently flawed, but because they are treated as rigid contracts rather than living hypotheses. The result is missed deadlines, wasted effort, eroded trust, and products that miss the market.\n\nA core reason is the absence of a clear strategy. Many roadmaps are feature lists disconnected from business goals or customer problems. Teams commit to shipping “the dashboard,” “the mobile app,” or “AI features” without defining success metrics or the underlying jobs-to-be-done. When priorities shift or data contradicts assumptions, the plan has no north star, so work continues on items that no longer matter.\n\nOvercommitment compounds the problem. Stakeholders demand dates and scope; product managers, seeking alignment, pack the timeline with more than capacity allows. Engineering realities—technical debt, unknowns, dependencies—are underestimated. The roadmap becomes a promise rather than a forecast, guaranteeing disappointment when delivery slips.\n\nPoor communication and lack of shared ownership accelerate failure. A roadmap created in isolation by product or leadership is rarely understood or believed by engineering, design, sales, or customers. Without regular, transparent updates and genuine input, it is ignored or gamed. Cross-functional dependencies surface too late, and political pressure to “hit the date” overrides learning.\n\nFinally, most roadmaps ignore change. Markets, competitors, user behavior, and technology evolve. Treating the document as static—rather than a set of bets that must be validated, killed, or adjusted—guarantees irrelevance. Teams that refuse to re-prioritize or cut scope when evidence arrives waste quarters on the original plan.\n\nSuccessful roadmaps invert these patterns: they start from outcomes, remain visibly tentative, involve the people who will execute them, and are revisited frequently with data. The ones that fail do the opposite. They confuse activity with progress and commitment with certainty. In a world of uncertainty, that is a recipe for waste.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n## Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nMost product roadmaps fail not because teams lack ambition or intelligence, but because roadmaps are often asked to do contradictory jobs. They are expected to predict the future, secure executive approval, coordinate departments, satisfy customers, and motivate employees—all while the underlying assumptions are still uncertain.\n\nThe first problem is that many roadmaps are really feature lists. They describe what a team intends to build rather than the customer problem it hopes to solve or the business result it wants to achieve. A roadmap might promise “launch reporting dashboard” or “add mobile notifications,” but neither statement explains why the work matters. When outputs become the focus, teams can deliver every item and still fail to improve retention, revenue, usability, or customer satisfaction.\n\nA second problem is false precision. Roadmaps often assign exact dates to work that depends on unknown technical constraints, changing priorities, customer research, and other teams’ decisions. These dates quickly become commitments, even when they began as guesses. Once a prediction is treated as a promise, teams optimize for being on schedule rather than learning what is valuable. They may ship low-impact features simply because those features appeared on an old plan.\n\nRoadmaps also fail when they are built as political documents. Leaders may use them to demonstrate progress, sales teams to make promises, and executives to allocate resources. Each group adds requests, until the roadmap becomes a compromise among stakeholders rather than a coherent strategy. The loudest customer or most senior executive can determine priorities, even when evidence suggests that another problem deserves attention.\n\nAnother weakness is that roadmaps often ignore discovery. Teams assume they already know what customers need, then move directly into delivery. As new information emerges, however, the plan becomes obsolete. A roadmap that cannot change is not a strategy; it is a constraint. Markets shift, competitors respond, regulations change, and experiments reveal that an appealing idea has little value.\n\nFinally, many organizations measure roadmap success by completion. This encourages activity instead of impact. Teams celebrate launching features but rarely examine whether those features changed user behavior or advanced company goals. Without explicit outcomes and feedback loops, failure remains hidden behind delivery metrics.\n\nEffective roadmaps are therefore less like train timetables and more like strategic hypotheses. They should communicate the problems worth solving, the outcomes expected, the evidence behind the priorities, and the conditions that would cause the plan to change. They should provide direction without pretending to offer certainty. Most roadmaps fail when they confuse planning with prediction. The best ones succeed by making uncertainty visible, aligning teams around outcomes, and preserving the flexibility to learn.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
